      trivia: "【Twenty Years from Trademark to Launch】Villazon & Co. registered the US trademark for Sancho Panza in 1981, but the non-Cuban cigar itself was not sold until 2001. Where Punch and Hoyo were made continuously from the 1960s and 1970s onwards, this marque passed twenty years existing only on paper — and, carrying that much less of the Cuban version's shadow, was designed from the start as a rich cigar for the American market.\n【Same Villazon Descent, Different Companies】In STG's 2021 division, El Rey del Mundo moved to the Forged Cigar Company, while Sancho Panza stayed with General Cigar alongside Punch, Hoyo de Monterrey, Macanudo, CAO and Cohiba. That brother marques of Villazon descent now sit in separate companies is a point frequently confused in practice.\n【The Box-press Abandoned in 2022】In the complete overhaul of July 2022 led by Matt Booth and Justin Andrews, the Original changed from box-pressed to round, with blends and livery replaced alike; the design is said to draw on the Latin culture of West Los Angeles. The same 'Sancho Panza Original' is a different cigar on either side of 2022.\n【From Spanish Cities to Common Nouns】The 2004 Extra Fuerte used Spanish city names — Madrid, Barcelona, Cordoba — while the standard line drew on 'Don Quixote' for Dulcinea, Glorioso, Valiente and Quixote. The 2022 renewal swept these away in favour of the generic Robusto, Toro and Gigante.\n【The Cuban Version Down to a Single Belicosos】The only Cuban Sancho Panza in year-round production today is the pirámide-shaped Belicosos (about 5 1/2 × 52); the Non Plus was officially discontinued in 2019. Less a great marque now than a vessel used from time to time for regional editions."
